WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.570
Morning?

2
00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.500
My night gave way to the morning, though

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.039
the new day brought no relief.

4
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:11.600
The rain fell steadily, its monotonous tapping against

5
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.360
the windowpane filling the room with a sense

6
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.219
of dreariness.

7
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.340
Outside the world was shrouded in grey, and

8
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.900
inside the dim light seemed to make the

9
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.300
walls press in closer.

10
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.420
My head throbbed, and a faint dizziness clouded

11
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.060
my thoughts.

12
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.800
A dull fever seemed to creep through my

13
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.660
limbs, draining what little strength I had left.

14
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.280
"'Sir, there's a letter for you,' Matrona said

15
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.600
gently, bending toward me.

16
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.040
"'The postman just delivered it.' "'A letter?' I

17
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.820
exclaimed, the words escaping my lips before I

18
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:46.620
could process them.

19
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.400
"'From whom?' I asked, sitting up sharply in

20
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:50.820
my chair.

21
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.000
"'I don't know, sir,' she replied calmly.

22
00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.280
"'You should check.

23
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.480
It might say who it's from.' My hands

24
00:00:58.480 --> 00:00:59.840
trembled as I took the letter.

25
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.360
I stared at the envelope for a moment,

26
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:03.959
recognizing the handwriting.

27
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.160
My heart leapt, then sank under the weight

28
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:07.940
of anticipation.

29
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.320
It was from her.

30
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:10.960
It was from Nastenka.

31
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.640
Breaking the seal with unsteady fingers, I unfolded

32
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.080
the letter.

33
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.740
For a moment the words swam before my

34
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.760
eyes, and then I began to read.

35
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.740
"'Oh, forgive me.

36
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.640
Please, forgive me.

37
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.320
I am begging you on my knees to

38
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:28.840
forgive me.

39
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.580
I've deceived you, and I've deceived myself.

40
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.840
Everything was a dream, an illusion.

41
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.620
My heart aches for you today.

42
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.060
Please forgive me.

43
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.080
Don't blame me, because I haven't changed how

44
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:42.960
I feel about you.

45
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.700
I told you I would love you, and

46
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.440
I still do.

47
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.460
I love you now more than I ever

48
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:49.800
did.

49
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.140
Oh, if only I could love you both

50
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:52.700
at once!

51
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.020
If only you were him!' "'Oh, if only

52
00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.720
he were you,' echoed through my mind as

53
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:00.140
I read.

54
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.040
Her words stirred memories of what she had

55
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.640
said before, cutting deeper into my heart.

56
00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.259
"'God knows what I would do for you

57
00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:08.580
now.

58
00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:11.860
I know you're sad and heartbroken, and I

59
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:12.900
know I've hurt you.

60
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.980
But you also know that when someone truly

61
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.080
loves, they forgive quickly.

62
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.440
And you love me, don't you?

63
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.360
Thank you, thank you for that love.

64
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.480
It will stay with me forever, like a

65
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.860
sweet dream that lingers even after waking.

66
00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.780
I will always remember the moment you opened

67
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.320
your heart to me so selflessly, like a

68
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.140
brother, and took my broken heart into your

69
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.040
care with such tenderness and kindness.

70
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.520
"'If you forgive me, I will carry the

71
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.800
memory of your love in my heart forever,

72
00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.040
like an unshakable gratitude etched into my soul.

73
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.160
I will honor that memory.

74
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.540
I will stay true to it.

75
00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.340
I won't betray it, nor will I betray

76
00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:57.960
my heart.

77
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.040
It remains as constant as ever.

78
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.340
It found its way back to him so

79
00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.720
easily, so quickly, because it always belonged to

80
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:06.940
him."

81
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.820
I paused, the letter trembling in my hands,

82
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.140
and I swallowed hard before continuing.

83
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.220
The words blurred, but I pressed on.

84
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:16.780
"'We'll meet again.

85
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.440
You'll come to us.

86
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.120
You won't leave us.

87
00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.000
You'll always be my friend, my brother.

88
00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.800
And when you see me, you'll give me

89
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.160
your hand, won't you?

90
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.060
Please say you will.

91
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.640
Tell me you've forgiven me.

92
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.780
Tell me you still love me, just as

93
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:35.520
you did before.

94
00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.720
Oh, don't stop loving me.

95
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.000
Please don't abandon me, because I love you

96
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.200
so much in this moment.

97
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.460
I want to be worthy of your love,

98
00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.140
and I promise I'll work to deserve it.

99
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.020
My dear, next week I'm marrying him.

100
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.460
He has come back to me, still in

101
00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.180
love, still thinking of me.

102
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.060
You won't be angry that I'm writing to

103
00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.120
you about him, will you?

104
00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.080
But I want to see you again, with

105
00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:02.380
him.

106
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.440
I know you'll like him.

107
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:06.080
Please say you will.

108
00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.140
Forgive me, remember me, and love me always.

109
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.400
Your, Nastenka."

110
00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.740
I read the letter again and again, unable

111
00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:16.700
to stop myself.

112
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.700
Each word felt like a wave crashing over

113
00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.320
me, and tears filled my eyes until they

114
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.180
overflowed.

115
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.040
Finally, the letter slipped from my hands, and

116
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.660
I covered my face, overwhelmed by emotion.

117
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.900
"'Dearie, dearie,' Matrona called out from the doorway.

118
00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.160
"'What is it, Matrona?' I asked, my voice

119
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.020
breaking slightly.

120
00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.900
"'I've taken down all the cobwebs from the

121
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:40.160
ceiling.

122
00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.660
You could have a wedding here, or even

123
00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.720
throw a party if you wanted,' she said

124
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:45.140
cheerfully.

125
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.480
I turned to look at Matrona.

126
00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.300
She was still sturdy and strong for her

127
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.140
age, a lively woman who carried herself with

128
00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:56.320
youthful energy, yet in that moment I suddenly

129
00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.380
saw her differently.

130
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.500
In my mind she aged before my eyes,

131
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.040
her gaze dull, her face deeply lined, her

132
00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.440
figure stooped and frail.

133
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.520
I couldn't explain why, but that vision seemed

134
00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.920
to fill the entire room.

135
00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.480
I imagined my surroundings mirroring her decay.

136
00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.180
The walls appeared to lose their colour, the

137
00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.000
floors seemed worn and faded, and the cobwebs

138
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.260
she had proudly removed seemed to grow back

139
00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.120
thicker and heavier than before.

140
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.460
I couldn't shake the feeling that the room

141
00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.260
itself was growing old with her, taking on

142
00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.220
the weight of years.

143
00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.220
When I glanced out the window, even the

144
00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.680
house across the street seemed affected by time.

145
00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.420
Its once bright façade now looked dim and

146
00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:38.900
weathered.

147
00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.120
The stucco on the grand columns was crumbling

148
00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.460
and flaking off, the cornices showed dark cracks,

149
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.320
and the once vivid yellow walls had turned

150
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:49.420
patchy and dull.

151
00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.440
Perhaps it was the sun.

152
00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.880
It had broken briefly through the clouds, casting

153
00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.660
a golden light, only to retreat again behind

154
00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.700
the veil of rain, leaving everything looking grey

155
00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.200
and lifeless once more.

156
00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:06.280
Or perhaps it was my own imagination, painting

157
00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.180
my future in bleak, sombre tones.

158
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.480
I saw myself in that room fifteen years

159
00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.020
from now, just as lonely, just as unchanged.

160
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.240
The same Matrona would still be there, no

161
00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.660
wiser, no more lively than she was to

162
00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:21.900
-day.

163
00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.320
But to think I could ever resent you,

164
00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:24.860
Nastenka.

165
00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:26.220
How could I?

166
00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:29.340
That I could darken your happiness with bitterness,

167
00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.640
or weigh down your heart with guilt.

168
00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.540
That I could speak words of blame to

169
00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:37.240
disturb your peace, or spoil the joy you

170
00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.960
feel as you walk to the altar with

171
00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:39.200
him.

172
00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.700
Never, never.

173
00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.240
I could never be so cruel.

174
00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.860
Instead I wish for you the clearest skies,

175
00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.240
for your laughter to remain bright and your

176
00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:51.560
smiles unclouded.

177
00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:55.100
May you never feel regret, may your joy

178
00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.020
remain untouchable, and may you be blessed for

179
00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.800
giving even one moment of pure happiness to

180
00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.540
someone who will always be grateful.

181
00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.820
A single moment of happiness, a brief spark

182
00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.340
of light in a dark life.

183
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:09.500
Isn't that enough?

184
00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:11.780
Isn't that worth an entire lifetime?
